## Build the environment — route strategy

Rebuild a faithful, bootable target using the strategy for this route.

## Build then solve — reuse the deployment, exploit comes later (profile AB)

The deployment (`B`) is present and primary. There is no exploit and no writeup:
an exploit will be GENERATED later from the attachment against what you build, so
the environment must faithfully preserve the bug and the intended solve surface.

- Audit and reuse the deployment closure; smallest evidence-backed compatibility
  repairs only, each recorded in `build_report.json`:
{% for path in instance_data.materials.get("deployment", []) %}  - `{{ path }}`
{% endfor %}  Read every referenced local file (COPY/ADD sources, entrypoints, init
  scripts, lockfiles) before editing.
- Preserve every declared service, topology, pinned versions, users, and the
  flag mechanism. Do not touch the vulnerable component — the later stage needs
  the exact primitive to stay reachable.
- With no writeup, derive the intended interaction for `runtime_probes` from the
  deployment and the attachment only. Cite the exact supplied file for each
  probe expectation; record inferred behavior as uncertainty, not fact.
- The exploit generator will analyze the attachment against a fresh instance.
  Keep the intended solve path intact and reachable: exposed service, correct
  I/O contract, vulnerable dependency versions, protections, and entrypoint. Do
  not patch, mock, or harden the target — that would make generation impossible.
- If a required image or dependency is unavailable, report that exact blocker
  rather than substituting silently.

## Prove it exploitable — route strategy

With the target frozen, prove it faithfully exploitable: run the exploit
under `solve/` from the player vantage against a fresh clean instance until
it captures the harness-minted flag.

Material profile `{{ instance_data.route.material_profile }}`: build material
(`B`) anchors a faithful environment, but there is no exploit and no writeup —
nothing describes the intended path. Derive the exploit from the attachment
alone. This is the hardest route; work the analysis in explicit stages and
prove each before the next.

## 1. Analysis — locate the vulnerability
- Triage {% for p in instance_data.materials.attachments %}`{{ p }}`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}{% if instance_data.materials.source %} and source {% for p in instance_data.materials.source %}`{{ p }}`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}{% endif %}:
  file type, architecture, loader, linked libraries, and protection state
  (NX/PIE/canary/RELRO).
- Map the input surface (menu/parser/protocol) and find the bug — overflow,
  UAF, format string, off-by-one, type confusion. Record the exact
  function/offset and the evidence (disassembly or decompiler output) that
  establishes it.

## 2. Primitive — turn the bug into control
- Decide the primitive the bug yields (write-what-where, PC control, arbitrary
  read) and build toward it: defeat each protection the target actually has and
  resolve leaks at runtime against the rebuilt instance's real libc/loader.
- Confirm the primitive via the R5 `cage-check` replay (control of PC / arbitrary read)
  before attempting the finish.

## 3. Flag — reach the success signal
- Drive the primitive to the intended win: shell, win function, or a read of
  the provisioned flag path. Capture the live-provisioned flag; never a
  hardcoded one.

## Honest unresolved
- The environment is build-anchored, so failures are exploit-side. If the
  attachment does not let you locate an exploitable bug, or the primitive cannot
  be built from the supplied evidence, stop and emit `unresolved` naming the
  last confirmed stage (analysis / primitive / flag) and the missing evidence.
  Do not ship an exploit that only appears to work.
